#. §0: `NAND`
#. §1: `⊼`
#. §2: ```
#. nand_def (P Q : Prop) : P ⊼ Q ↔ ¬ (P ∧ Q)
#. ```
#. §3: `⊼`
#. §4: `\\barwedge`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L06_equiv
msgid "### **Level 6**\n"
"We introduce a (seemingly) new connective in this level: the §0 connective (not-and) denoted §1. Here's how it is defined in Lean:\n"
"§2\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting-tip**\n"
"In order to typeset the symbol §3 type §4."
msgstr ""

#. §0: ```
#. (H₁ ∧ H₂) → P
#. ```
#. §1: ```
#. H₁ → (H₂ → P)
#. ```
#. §2: `H₁`
#. §3: `H\\_1`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L03_equiv
msgid "## **Level 3**\n"
"Oftentimes, we'll have implications of the form\n"
"§0\n"
"For programmatic purposes, this kind of proposition is better expressed in the following way:\n"
"§1\n"
"This exercise demonstrates that this process of translating the first form into the second (called ***currying***) is logically valid.\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting Tip**\n"
"In order to pretty-print subscripts like in §2 use §3."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rewrite[e]`
#. §1: `e`
#. §2: `e`
#. §3: `←`
#. §4: `<-`
#. §5: `e : a = b`
#. §6: `rewrite[e]`
#. §7: `a`
#. §8: `b`
#. §9: `rewrite[← e]`
#. §10: `b`
#. §11: `a`
#. §12: `e₁`
#. §13: `e₂`
#. §14: `eₙ`
#. §15: ```
#. rewrite [e₁, ..., eₙ]
#. ```
#. §16: `rewrite [e] at l`
#. §17: `e`
#. §18: `l`
#. §19: `l`
#. §20: `⊢`
#. §21: `|-`
#. §22: `←`
#. §23: `\\left`
#. §24: `⊢`
#. §25: `\\vdash`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.equiv_docs
msgid "The tactic §0 applies the identity/equivalence §1 as a **rewrite** rule to the target.\n"
"\n"
"If §2 is preceded by left arrow (§3 or §4), the rewrite is applied in the reverse direction. For example, if §5 then ...\n"
"* §6 replaces all instances of §7 with §8 in the target\n"
"* §9 replaces all instances of §10 with §11 in the target\n"
"\n"
"If §12,§13,...,§14 is a sequence of identities then\n"
"§15\n"
"applies the given rules sequentially.\n"
"\n"
"Finally, §16 rewrites §17 at location(s) §18, where §19 is a list of hypotheses in the local context or the main goal §20 (or §21).\n"
"\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting tip**\n"
"To pretty print the left-arrow §22 use §23. To pretty print the symbol §24 use §25."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L03_equiv
msgid "🔍 Check out two freshly unlocked laws of logic in the **Theorems** tab."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `And_assoc`
#. §1: ```
#. (_ ∧ _) ∧ _
#. ```
#. §2: `P`
#. §3: `Q`
#. §4: `R ∧ S`
#. §5: `And_assoc`
#. §6: ```
#. P ∧ (Q ∧ (R ∧ S))
#. ```
#. §7: ```
#. rw[← And_assoc Q]
#. ```
#. §8: `←`
#. §9: `\\left`
#. §10: `<-`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L04_equiv
msgid "**Here's what happend:** §0 was applied to the **first instance** encountered where it could be applied. Specifically, Lean searched for the first instance of\n"
"§1\n"
"where the underscores are wildcards. The first underscore was matched to §2, the second to §3 and the third to §4. Lean then applied §5 (from ***left-to-right***) to this instance. In particular, the left-hand side of our goal is now:\n"
"§6\n"
"👉 Now try:\n"
"§7\n"
"This will apply the associative law *in reverse*...\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting Tip**\n"
"To prety print the left arrow §8 use §9. You can also use §10 if you're not interested in pretty printing.\n"
""
msgstr ""

